{"id": 38, "ts": 1780645118.4520984, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 3, "agent": "sarah_mitchell", "message": "I appreciate Emily\u2019s shift toward Candidate\u202fC, especially given the clear evidence that C \u201ckeeps calm in a crisis,\u201d \u201cmakes correct decisions quickly,\u201d and \u201ccreates a positive atmosphere with his crew.\u201d Those points directly address decision quality under pressure and crew cooperation\u2014criteria we have been missing for Candidate\u202fB.\nHowever, the communication concerns for C are substantial: he \u201chas difficulty communicating ideas,\u201d is \u201cregarded as egocentric,\u201d and is \u201cnot very willing to further his education.\u201d In a long\u2011distance cockpit, clear, respectful communication and openness to feedback are non\u2011negotiable for safety. Those traits could undermine situational awareness and crew resource management, outweighing the benefits of his calmness and reliability.\nCandidate\u202fB, while we lack concrete examples of crew\u2011cooperation and decision\u2011making under pressure, demonstrates strong, documented strengths: he is \u201cvery conscientious\u201d (operational reliability), \u201chandles stress very well\u201d (stress resilience), is \u201cgood at assessing weather conditions,\u201d and has \u201cexcellent computer skills\u201d (technical competence). The noted issues\u2014pretentiousness, occasional wrong tone, and a weak memory for numbers\u2014are more readily mitigated through targeted onboarding and communication coaching than the deeper interpersonal deficits observed for C.\nGiven the balance of evidence, I remain convinced that Candidate\u202fB is the safer provisional choice, provided we obtain at least one concrete example of his crew\u2011cooperation before finalisation."}
